## Proof

**Yes**, such an uncountable set of sets exists.

**Construction.** For each ordinal $\alpha$, let $\omega_\alpha$ denote the $\alpha$-th *initial ordinal* — the least ordinal whose cardinality is $\aleph_\alpha$. (So $\omega_0 = \omega$, $\omega_1$ is the least uncountable ordinal, etc.) Define

$$\mathcal{F} \;=\; \{\,\omega_\alpha : \alpha < \omega_1\,\}.$$

**$\mathcal{F}$ is a set.** The class function $\alpha \mapsto \omega_\alpha$ is defined on the set $\omega_1$. By the Axiom of Replacement, its image $\mathcal{F}$ is a set.

**$\mathcal{F}$ is uncountable.** The map $\alpha \mapsto \omega_\alpha$ is strictly increasing: the cardinalities $\aleph_\alpha$ are strictly increasing in $\alpha$ (by definition $\aleph_{\alpha+1}$ is the least cardinal above $\aleph_\alpha$, and at limits $\aleph_\lambda = \sup_{\alpha<\lambda}\aleph_\alpha > \aleph_\alpha$ for each $\alpha < \lambda$), so distinct $\alpha$ give distinct, hence strictly larger, initial ordinals. Therefore the map is injective and

$$|\mathcal{F}| = |\omega_1| = \aleph_1,$$

which is uncountable.

**The members of $\mathcal{F}$ have pairwise distinct cardinalities.** The cardinality of $\omega_\alpha$ is $\aleph_\alpha$, and since the sequence $(\aleph_\alpha)_{\alpha < \omega_1}$ is strictly increasing, no two distinct members $\omega_\alpha, \omega_\beta$ ($\alpha \neq \beta$) have the same cardinality.

**Conclusion.** $\mathcal{F}$ is an uncountable set of sets, and every two members of $\mathcal{F}$ have distinct cardinalities. Hence such a family exists. $\blacksquare$

